he Great around 1347."}, {"sourceSlug": "jarosław-of-bogoria-and-skotnik", "sourceName": "Jarosław of Bogoria and Skotnik", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "gniezno", "targetName": "Gniezno, Poland", "context": "Jarosław served as the Archbishop of Gniezno, making the city the primary seat of his ecclesiastical and administrative activities from 1342 to 1376."}, {"sourceSlug": "medieval-poland", "sourceName": "Medieval Poland", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "jarosław-of-bogoria-and-skotnik", "targetName": "Jarosław of Bogoria and Skotnik", "context": "Jarosław of Bogoria and Skotnik was a prominent figure within the political and ecclesiastical landscape of Medieval Poland during the 14th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Gniezno, Poland", "role": "Archiepiscopal See"}, {"name": "Kraków, Poland", "role": "University Foundation"}, {"name": "Skotniki, Poland", "role": "Family Estate"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jarosław of Bogoria and Skotnik", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jarosław-of-bogoria-and-skotnik"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q925554", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaros%C5%82aw_Bogoria_of_Skotniki"], "tags": ["Poland", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Jarosław of Bogoria and Skotnik's efforts were pivotal in establishing the Cracow Academy, an institution that became a cornerstone of Polish intellectual life, and in codifying Polish law, which provided a stable legal framework for the kingdom.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-16T07:03:37Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31931329751", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jarosław Bogoria of Skotniki was a Polish nobleman and bishop, member of the Bogoriowie family of the Bogorya.", "newValue": "Jarosław Bogoria of Skotniki (d. September 25, 1376) emerged as a towering ecclesiastical and political figure in 14th-century Poland, serving as Archbishop of Gniezno and Primate of Poland from 1342 until his death. A trusted advisor and close collaborator with King Casimir III the Great, his career spanned a pivotal era of state-building and cultural flourishing within the Kingdom of Poland, solidifying the monarch's authority and fostering institutional development. His influence extended across ecclesiastical reform, legal codification, and the establishment of the Cracow Academy, marking him as a central architect of medieval Polish identity and power.

As Archbishop, Jarosław meticulously reformed the ecclesiastical administration, standardizing diocesan structures and strengthening the financial independence of the Church through extensive land acquisitions and tithe collection. He played a crucial role in the codification of Polish law under Casimir the Great, contributing to the Statues of Wiślica and Piotrków (c. 1347), which unified legal practices across the kingdom. His most enduring contribution, however, was his instrumental involvement in the founding of the Cracow Academy in 1364, the second oldest university in Central Europe, designed to educate administrators, lawyers, and theologians for the burgeoning Polish state.

